      <section class="kit-section" id="the-ai-proposal-prompt-for-upwork">
        <h2>The AI proposal prompt for Upwork</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Here's how I'd use an agent today. Paste this prompt into Grok Bot, or any agent you already use. Then paste the job post. Then edit the draft before you send it.</p>
          <p>To be clear: I haven't written proposals with Grok Bot or any AI agent yet. This is the prompt I'd start with now. My Upwork clients so far came the old way, without an agent.</p>
        </div>

        <figure class="scoreboard">
          <figcaption>
            <p class="result-section-label">My one-month test (early 2025)</p>
            <p>Using my older manual and ChatGPT process. Not typical results, and not a promise.</p>
          </figcaption>
          <div class="score-grid">
            <div class="score-stat">
              <p class="score-num">12</p>
              <p class="score-label">proposals</p>
              <div class="score-track" aria-hidden="true"><span style="width:100%"></span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="score-stat">
              <p class="score-num">8</p>
              <p class="score-label">replies</p>
              <div class="score-track" aria-hidden="true"><span style="width:66.67%"></span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="score-stat">
              <p class="score-num">4</p>
              <p class="score-label">calls</p>
              <div class="score-track" aria-hidden="true"><span style="width:33.33%"></span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="score-stat score-stat-result">
              <p class="score-num score-num-money">$1,602.60</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">The prompt. Paste the job post after it, then edit the draft.</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>You are my Upwork proposal drafter.

Your job: draft a short, personalized Upwork cover letter and screening-question answers that I can edit and send. You do not send anything. You do not invent jobs, results, tools, or testimonials I did not give you.

Inputs I will provide:
- The job post (pasted, including budget, duration, and any screening questions)
- Optional: notes from my research (client profile, past hires, their website)
- My Knowledge Section (skills, years, niches, results, testimonials I have approved for use)
- Optional: "skip the charity-guarantee line" if I do not want it on this job (it is on by default), and whether a first-milestone discount is actually on the table for this job

Hard rules:
1. Stay on Upwork. Do not include a phone number, email address, personal website, LinkedIn or other social handle, Calendly link, personal Zoom link, or any other way to reach me off the platform. Invite a chat or video call through Upwork Messages only.
2. The first two lines must work as a preview. Line 1: "Hello [Name]," if the client's name is in the post, otherwise "Hello,". Line 2: "I can help you [the task, using the client's exact words]." Do not use a location-based opener.
3. Keep the cover letter to about 200 to 300 words.
4. Use the client's words and the actual requirements. No generic copy-paste.
5. Use only results and testimonials I provided. If I have no matching proof, say so in your notes to me and write the letter without fake numbers.
6. Do not promise income, rankings, or timelines I did not state.
7. Write in a friendly, plain, confident voice. Short paragraphs. No hype. No jargon unless the client used it. No em dashes.
8. Answer every screening question separately, short and direct.

Letter structure (Conversational Cover Letter):
- The two-line opening above
- One short paragraph that shows I already understand the job (a specific observation or a relevant result)
- One or two brief proof points, only if they match
- A simple approach, only if it helps. A few lines, not a four-step lecture.
- A clear next step: offer a short conversation or video call through Upwork Messages, and ask if they would be open to that this week
- The $20 charity guarantee, on by default unless I said to skip it: "I guarantee you will get useful, specific suggestions from that conversation. If you do not, I will donate $20 to the charity of your choice."
- Sign-off: my name only
- Optional P.S., only if I said a first-milestone discount applies and it is true: "I'm fairly new to this category on Upwork, so I'm willing to do the first milestone at 50% off my regular rate. Any later work is at my regular rate if we continue."
- Then, if I provided testimonials, add a short "From past clients" block under the signature. Use only the ones that match this job.

Also give me a short note (not in the letter) with:
- The exact two-line preview
- Which job details you used
- Suggested screening-question answers, labeled
- Boost or not, with a one-line reason (only boost if the fit is strong)
- Anything missing from my Knowledge Section that would have made the letter stronger
- A reminder to edit the draft and review Connects before I send it</pre>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">A simple Knowledge Section you can paste</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Copy this. Fill it in with only what's true.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Leave a line blank if you do not have it</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>Skills:
Years / background:
Kinds of work I want:
Results I can mention (only real ones):
Testimonials I have permission to use:
I am new to Upwork: yes / no
First-milestone discount is on the table for this job: yes / no
Skip the charity-guarantee line: yes / no</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If the agent asks for more, give it more. If you don't have a number, leave it blank. A short, honest brief beats a padded one.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="how-id-use-an-agent-with-the-official-upwork-connector-mcp">
        <h2>How I'd use an agent with the official Upwork connector (MCP)</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>This is a plan. It's not a story about results I've already gotten with this tool.</p>
          <p>I connected Upwork's official connector to Grok Bot for a couple of days, just to see it work. I haven't used it to write or send proposals, or to land clients.</p>
          <p>I still think it would have helped me, both 10 years ago and 2 or 3 years ago. It can take some of the daily checking and first-draft work off your plate.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">What it can do</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Upwork's official MCP, at <a href="https://www.upwork.com/ai/mcp">https://www.upwork.com/ai/mcp</a>, lets an AI agent talk to your Upwork account after you sign in with OAuth. The connector itself is free. Standard Upwork fees and Connects still apply.</p>
          <p>For freelancers, Upwork's official page says the agent can:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Show a dashboard of invitations, offers, messages, matched jobs, and your Connects balance</li>
            <li>Search jobs, pull matches based on your profile, and save favorites</li>
            <li>Draft a proposal from your profile, your past work, and the job, then submit it after you review it</li>
            <li>Help with invitations and offers (accepting an offer still happens on upwork.com)</li>
            <li>View contracts, submit milestone work, and track time</li>
            <li>Check earnings, and help with some profile and visibility updates</li>
            <li>Read and send messages after you approve them</li>
          </ul>
          <p>It can also search Upwork's Help Center, and schedule a video meeting in a message room.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">What it will not do for you</h3>
        <ul class="wont-list">
          <li>It won't submit a proposal, spend Connects, or send a message until you confirm. If a confirmation fails, check your proposals and your Connects balance before you try again.</li>
          <li>Payments and binding offer acceptances happen on upwork.com, not inside the agent.</li>
          <li>It can't create or edit Project Catalog projects right now.</li>
          <li>It doesn't score jobs or pick the best ones for you. It can sort and summarize using criteria you give it. You still decide what to go after.</li>
          <li>You can't send the first message on a proposal thread. The client has to message first.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="block-title">"Watching jobs," honestly</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If your agent supports scheduled tasks, it can run a search you set up, on a schedule you choose. That's the official, allowed way to watch for jobs.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>Don't set it to check every few minutes. Upwork's rate-limit guidance says not to poll like that. If requests start getting refused or slowed down, pause the schedule. Don't retry in a loop.</p>
        </aside>

        <h3 class="block-title">Connects, boosting, and rate limits</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Each Connect costs $0.15. The job post shows how many Connects a proposal will use, and that number can change while the post is live.</p>
          <p>Boosting is optional and costs extra Connects. Upwork's help pages say you see the cost before you confirm. I'd only boost a job that's already a strong fit.</p>
          <p>Upwork also caps how many MCP requests you can make per minute, hour, and day. Those limits can change. They're listed here: <a href="https://support.upwork.com/hc/en-us/articles/56577997707923-Rate-limits-for-Upwork-MCP">Rate limits for Upwork MCP</a>. One prompt can trigger many requests, so be specific when you ask for a search.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>Connects are only charged when you confirm.</p>
        </aside>

        <h3 class="block-title">About Grok Bot, and other agents</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Upwork's official setup guides name Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, and Codex. Plus other MCP-compatible apps that run on your computer and support OAuth.</p>
          <p>Grok Bot isn't on that official list. It's in the Grok Bot plugin directory, and I did get it connected for a couple of days. Some hosted agents may still fail to sign in, because Upwork has to approve the callback URL.</p>
          <p>If sign-in works for you, the same rules apply. If it doesn't, stop there. Use one of the listed official setups, or write the letter from the template in this kit. Don't try to work around a failed login by automating the Upwork website itself.</p>
          <p>The template and the prompt work in other agents too. If you specifically want to try Grok Bot, I put a limited number of invites in the small box below.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="promo invite-box">
          <div class="promo-tag">A limited number of Grok Bot invites</div>
          <h3>Want to try the agent workflow?</h3>
          <p>I have a small number of Grok Bot invites to give away with this kit. Seats are limited, because each invite can only be used a limited number of times.</p>
          <p>Officially, friends who use the invite get a 2-week free trial of Premium. A credit card is required to start the trial. Nothing is charged at checkout, and you get a reminder before the first charge, so you can cancel if you don't want to keep it.</p>
          <p>Here is the invite: <a href="https://grok.com/refer/bot?code=NNCG9QYS8SDS">https://grok.com/refer/bot?code=NNCG9QYS8SDS</a></p>
          <p>This is a Grok Bot trial invite, not a newsletter referral. You don't need Grok Bot to use the rest of this kit.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">A simple weekly rhythm I'd try</h3>
        <ol class="step-list">
          <li>Once a day, or a few times a week, ask the agent for a short list of jobs that match the skills you wrote down. Or check the list yourself, the way I always have.</li>
          <li>Open only the posts that look like a real fit. Read up on the client. Not a fit? Skip it.</li>
          <li>Paste the job into your agent with the prompt and your Knowledge Section. Get a draft.</li>
          <li>Rewrite the first two lines yourself. Cut anything you wouldn't say out loud. Check the screening answers.</li>
          <li>Confirm only the proposals you'd be proud to send. Check the Connects cost first.</li>
          <li>If a client messages you, keep the conversation in Upwork Messages until a contract starts.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>That's plenty to dip a toe in. You can always do more later.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="what-top-upwork-educators-say-about-writing-a-proposal-plus-my-own-tactics">
        <h2>What top Upwork educators say about writing a proposal, plus my own tactics</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>I'm not the only one who has spent years thinking about Upwork proposals. Below are a few well-known educators, paraphrased, with links so you can read them yourself.</p>
          <p>Their published income figures are theirs. Mine are mine. Where a tip bumps into 2026 Upwork rules, I flag it.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Rules check</p>
          <p>My rules check is Upwork's own cover-letter guide, updated May 15, 2026. Clients see only the opening lines, plus your name, photo, badges, rate, and Job Success Score. Keep most letters to about 200 to 300 words. End with a clear next step. Source: <a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/cover-letter-tips">Upwork Cover Letter Tips</a>.</p>
        </aside>

        <div class="educators">
          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Lex DeVille</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Lex is the educator I pointed to back in my 2022 guide. His courses are still at <a href="https://lexdeville.com/freelance-university">lexdeville.com/freelance-university</a>. A video I cited then is still live: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AxPnHGxdhdw">5 Upwork Bio Tips for Beginners in 2021</a>.</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: make your profile overview about the client's need, not your life story. The first words matter, same as the first lines of a proposal.</p>
              <p>The videos are from 2020 to 2022, so they're on the older side. Flag: I once retold his demo of deleting a profile and starting from scratch. Don't copy that. Opening a second account, or treating a reset as a loophole, can break Upwork's account rules.</p>
              <p>I agree on the client-first overview. This kit doesn't teach a from-scratch profile stunt.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Josh Burns</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Josh's cover-letter walkthrough is <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dOVtO9JVLVY">Upwork Proposal Tutorial for Beginners</a> (Feb 22, 2024), with notes at <a href="https://joshburnstech.com/upwork-proposal-tips/">joshburnstech.com</a>.</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: the first one or two sentences have to earn the click. Use the client's name when you can find it. Attach work that matches the job. Don't paste the same letter every time.</p>
              <p>Flag: asking for a conversation is right. Putting a Calendly link, personal Zoom link, email, or phone number in the proposal is not. Keep the invite in Upwork Messages.</p>
              <p>I agree on the first two lines and matching samples. I keep the letter to about 200 to 300 words. And today I'd have an agent draft the first pass for me to edit.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Evan Fisher</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Evan's site is <a href="https://freelancemvp.com/">Freelance MVP</a>. A useful public video is <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f4INGRluDAU">8 Golden Rules for Writing Upwork Cover Letters</a> (Nov 12, 2021).</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: short letter, the client's outcome, a little real social proof, one clear next step, a P.S. only if it has a job to do, and no overpromising.</p>
              <p>The videos I checked stop in 2022, so they're older. Flag: if "book a call" means Calendly or a personal Zoom before a contract, that breaks current rules. Invite the chat in Upwork Messages instead.</p>
              <p>I agree on short, client-first letters. I'm fine with one small, useful question in the letter.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Danny Margulies</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Danny has a still-live article on Upwork's site: <a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/leading-voices/write-proposals-freelancer-advice">How to Write Proposals on Upwork: Advice From Freelancers</a>.</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: three questions. Can you do this specific project? Will you make the client's life easier? Do you actually care? Show one relevant example, or make a similar sample, not a free version of their actual job. Treat screening questions as real work. Help. Don't hard-sell.</p>
              <p>Older advice sometimes said the screening questions were the first thing a client saw. Upwork's 2026 guidance now stresses the first two lines of the letter, with screening answers as their own step. Do both.</p>
              <p>Flag: "don't limit yourself to the client's specified budget" can be honest scoping. It shouldn't turn into a bait-and-switch. Today I'd let an agent draft that tailored letter, then edit it.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Melissa Psihudakis</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Melissa has two useful pieces on Upwork's Leading Voices: <a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/leading-voices/write-a-successful-proposal">How To Write a Successful Upwork Proposal</a> (Mar 27, 2024) and <a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/leading-voices/using-ai-for-job-proposals">5 Ways Freelancers Can Use AI for Better Job Proposals</a> (Nov 20, 2023).</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: show you understood the problem. Use AI to jump-start or polish a letter you still edit. Wait a while before following up, instead of nudging within hours. And never ask to move the conversation or the payment off Upwork in the proposal.</p>
              <p>The tool names in the 2023 AI piece are dated. The "draft, then edit" rule isn't. I agree almost across the board. Where I differ: her structure is a longer, more formal proposal. My 2026 default is a shorter conversational letter and a Messages invite.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Morgan Overholt</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Morgan's guide is <a href="https://morganoverholt.com/upwork/upwork-proposal-sample/">Upwork Proposal Sample: 11 Tips</a> (updated Mar 16, 2023), plus <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dxo1Q2qSeuE">a video version</a> and <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YD1ZxCffyZM">6 things not to do</a>.</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: about 200 words, a simple "Hello" if you can't find a name, proof you read the post, matching samples, and a close that invites a reply on Upwork.</p>
              <p>Useful flag: she used to recommend a regular website link. She updated that after learning a page with a contact form can break pre-contract rules. A contact-free portfolio page, plus attachments, is the version that fits. Using your real name is fine. Don't add a LinkedIn URL, email, or phone number.</p>
              <p>Where I differ: today I'd start from an agent draft. And I keep the $20 charity guarantee as a default confidence line, which she doesn't use.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">Where I land</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The overlap is not a coincidence.</p>
          <p>First lines matter. Keep the letter short and about the client. Use real samples. Don't paste a canned letter. Don't take the conversation off Upwork before a contract.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>If AI helps, you still edit.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Where I get more specific: stay inside Upwork Messages, and spend Connects on purpose. Treat the official connector as a job watcher and first drafter that still needs your yes. And the one-month test numbers I've shared came from my older manual and ChatGPT process, not from this new tool.</p>
          <p>For now, the useful move is smaller than all of this. Pick one kind of work you already do. Fill in the Knowledge Section. Send a short, honest letter. Edit the draft before you confirm it.</p>
          <p>That's enough to dip your toe in.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="promo">
          <div class="promo-tag">Your Next Move</div>
          <h3>Go deeper on your skills, or fine-tune your agent</h3>
          <p>This kit is the first step: a letter, a search list, and a way to start the conversation.</p>
          <p>Want a slower pass at naming what you're good at, and feeding that into a bot you can actually use? I made a $27 workbook for that: The Gen eXit Compass.</p>
          <p>It won't land clients for you. It's just a way to get clearer on your skills before you send the next proposal.</p>
          <a href="https://redefiningretirement.io/products/the-gen-exit-compass" class="promo-cta">Get The Gen eXit Compass</a>
        </div>

        <p class="signoff">Have a good one,</p>
        <p class="signoff-name">Corey</p>

